"An OAuth authorization diagram shows protocol roles, browser redirects, code exchange, and token use for a selected grant.\n\nUse when: Review trust boundaries in delegated API authorization. Explain authorization-code and PKCE interactions in an application.\n\nAvoid when: The question is only static identity federation trust configuration.\n\nVisual grammar: Lanes distinguish resource owner, client, authorization server, and resource server. Messages label redirects, authorization codes, token requests, and protected-resource calls. Annotations identify state, PKCE challenge/verifier, audience, and token confidentiality boundaries.\n\nBuild: Name the grant, client type, and current protocol profile. Draw front-channel redirects separately from back-channel exchanges. Mark validation and token-handling responsibilities. Use distinct message lanes and short placeholder values rather than actual credentials.\n\nCheck: Authorization is not described as authentication without the relevant identity protocol. Tokens are shown only at intended recipients and channels. Redirect, state, and PKCE checks match the selected profile. Boundary lines and validation notes never cover protocol messages.\n\nPitfalls: Placing real tokens or secrets in an explanatory diagram. Treating a browser redirect as a confidential server-to-server exchange.\n\nExample: A public client sends a code challenge, receives a code, and exchanges it with the verifier before calling an API.\n\nAlternatives: Sequence diagram, Identity federation diagram."
